  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: S&EDisc 2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "You\u2019re talking with a patient advocate who is visiting a research
    lab for the first time. She asks about your research team\u2019s early tests
    and experiments in the lab, and you share that your team has identified a potential
    cancer drug that will be moving into animal studies soon. \u201CThat\u2019s awesome!\u201D
    she replies, \u201CSo, now that the research is moving into animal studies, the
    institutional review board (IRB) must review and approve it before the research
    can continue, right?\u201D\n\nIs this true or false?"
  explanation: An Institutional Review Board (IRB) is a committee that reviews and
    approves research that involves humans, because its main job is to protect people
    who volunteer for research studies. Because early discovery work does not involve
    humans, it does not require IRB review. Animal studies are reviewed and approved
    by a different oversight group called an Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee
    (IACUC). IRBs become involved later, before any research involving patient participants
    can begin.
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Discovery
